<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Batoche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batoche">Batoche</a>, fought on 9–12 May 1885 in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, was the decisive battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a>. Canadian troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-middleton">Frederick Middleton</a> overran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> settlement held by the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-dumont">Gabriel Dumont</a>. Riel surrendered days later and was tried and hanged for treason in Regina in November 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-batoche.jpg" alt="Battle of Batoche" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Seargent Grundy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Batoche_Print_by_Seargent_Grundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batoche">Batoche</a> had been founded in 1872 by Xavier Letendre dit Batoche where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> freighters crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-saskatchewan-river">South Saskatchewan River</a>, and about 50 families had claimed river lots there by 1884. Anxiety over land claims and a changing economy led to resistance, and the village became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>'s Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>. Its defenders dug trenches and hidden rifle pits around it. Wary after the government's setbacks at Duck Lake, Fish Creek and Cut Knife, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-middleton">Frederick Middleton</a> approached slowly, planning to land troops behind the town from the steamboat Northcote, but on 9 May the Métis lowered the ferry cable, which tore away the boat's masts and smokestacks and sent it drifting out of the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 9 May the militia advanced past the church at Mission Ridge and came under fire from rifle pits it could not see. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> rush on the artillery was stopped only by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling gun</a> of Lieutenant Arthur L. Howard, on leave from the Connecticut National Guard. Shaken by the resistance, Middleton pulled his men back that evening to a zareba, an improvised fortified camp. On 10 May he shelled the town, and on 11 May a feint to the north showed how thinly the defenders were spread.</p><p>By 12 May three-quarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> were wounded or scattered and the rest were so short of ammunition that some fired nails and stones. After Colonel Bowen van Straubenzee's column failed to attack on time that morning, the Midland Battalion and the Royal Grenadiers, with the 90th Winnipeg Rifles, made a charge in the afternoon that no one is known to have ordered, and stormed the village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Riel surrendered on 15 May. Gabriel Dumont fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, returning in 1893, and is buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batoche">Batoche</a>. Poundmaker and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/big-bear">Big Bear</a> received prison terms. The rank-and-file fighters were granted an amnesty, but murders committed outside the fighting were punished, and eight men were hanged in November 1885, the largest mass hanging in Canadian history. Middleton reported 8 Canadian and 51 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> dead, but modern historians generally accept 12 dead and 20 wounded on the Métis side. Batoche is now a national historic site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Batoche” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Wolseley Expedition to Red River in 1870 after the resistance led by Louis Riel, and the suppression of the North-West Rebellion of 1885, when troops moved west on the unfinished Canadian Pacific Railway defeated the Métis at the <b>Battle of Batoche</b>; Riel was hanged later that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li></ul></section>
